 "get_file_summary": {
  "acl": null,
  "content": "---\nlab_source_id: source-ac72293581f1a365\ntitle: Dsup, a nucleosome-binding protein from Ramazzottius varieornatus, suppresses hydroxyl-radical damage to DNA\nstable_identifier: 10.5555/synthetic.dsup.2016.001\ncanonical_url: \npublished_at: 2016-09-20\nsource_type: journal_article\nvenue: Journal of Extremophile Biology\nlicense: cc-by\nauthors: Hiroshi Tanaka, Mei Sato, Kenji Ito\nprovider: fixtures\n---\n\n# Dsup, a nucleosome-binding protein from Ramazzottius varieornatus, suppresses hydroxyl-radical damage to DNA\n\nWe characterise Dsup, a damage suppressor protein isolated from the tardigrade Ramazzottius varieornatus. Dsup binds nucleosomes directly and coats chromatin in a sequence-independent manner. In an in vitro hydroxyl-radical footprinting assay, Dsup reduced double-strand break formation by 42 percent relative to protein-free controls. Human HEK293 cells stably expressing Dsup showed a 38 percent reduction in gamma-H2AX foci after 4 Gy of X-irradiation. The protective effect scaled with Dsup occupancy and was abolished by mutating the C-terminal basic region, indicating that shielding, rather than repair induction, is the mechanism.",
